      en: "The Malagarasi Swamps are vast wetlands spanning about 8,000 km² along the Malagarasi River, straddling Burundi and Tanzania. They are famous for being a key habitat of the shoebill stork (Balaeniceps rex), a prehistoric-looking bird standing up to 1.4 meters tall. The swamps consist of papyrus reeds, water lilies, and open channels. They support a rich biodiversity, including hippos, crocodiles, and over 200 bird species. The area is a Ramsar site, protected since 2012. Local communities rely on fishing and papyrus harvesting. Annual rainfall averages 1,000 mm. Water levels vary dramatically between wet and dry seasons.",
